    "result": "Perfect! The bug is fixed. Here's what I found and fixed:\n\n## Root Cause\nThe issue was a **floating-point precision error**. When converting \"39.95\" to cents by multiplying by 100, JavaScript produces `3995.0000000000005` instead of exactly `3995` due to how binary floating-point numbers are represented. Netflix (12.99) and Spotify (10.99) work fine because they happen to be exactly representable in binary floating-point.\n\n## The Fix\nI wrapped the price conversions with `Math.round()` to handle floating-point imprecision:\n\n1. **In `parseEuros()`** (line 13): Changed from `Number(...).toFixed(2)) * 100` to `Math.round(Number(...) * 100)`\n2. **In `decimalCommaToCents()` path** (line 14): Added `Math.round()` wrapper\n3. **In `cellCents()`** (line 25): Changed from `Number(s) * 100` to `Math.round(Number(s) * 100)` for CSV import\n\nAll 50 tests pass, and your gym membership now adds successfully with any normal price!",
